  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 157 PM EDT FRI OCT 23 2026\n\nAmplified upper ridging over the Mississippi and Ohio River\nValleys Sunday and Monday will gradually weaken and get shunted \nsouth and east of the area, opening eastern Kentucky to more \nwesterly and/or west- southwesterly flow aloft mid to late next \nweek. At the surface, high pressure will remain in control through\nmuch if not all of the period. A cold front may move into the \nMidwest and Great Lakes region by Wednesday and Thursday of next \nweek, allowing for surface winds to increase out of the south to \nsouthwesterly, though wind speeds would remain light. \n\nSunny/clear skies and dry weather is expected through the period \nwith mild days and cool, if not chilly, nights, with valley fog\nthrough the river valleys and patchy frost (at least for Sunday\nnight and perhaps Monday night) in some sheltered valley \nlocations removed from larger bodies of water. The one potential \nexception is around the middle of next week (i.e., Tuesday night \nthrough Wednesday) when most models depict a weakening shortwave \ncrossing the area, bringing an increase in clouds. Such a scenario\nwould likely bring warmer overnight lows and reduce ridge/valley \nsplits in temperatures.",
